Add MAP_JIT for the iOS Simulator so the VM can reserve executable memory.

The mobile runtime strips MAP_JIT for every __IOS__ build:

    const int flags = MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_NORESERVE | MAP_ANONYMOUS
    #ifdef __IOS__
        ;
    #else
        MACOS_ONLY(| (exec ? MAP_JIT : 0));
    #endif

That is right for a device -- iOS has no MAP_JIT and does not need one -- but the
Simulator is not iOS. It is an arm64 macOS process wearing an iOS SDK, and Apple
Silicon macOS refuses an executable mapping that was not requested with MAP_JIT.
HotSpot therefore fails during initialisation, before anything else runs:

    Error occurred during initialization of VM
    Could not reserve enough space in CodeCache (4096K)

The reported size is whatever InitialCodeCacheSize happens to be, which is why it
looks like a sizing problem; it is not. 256K fails exactly as readily as 8M, and
the com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit entitlement does not help on its own, because
the entitlement only permits MAP_JIT -- it does not imply it.

TARGET_OS_SIMULATOR distinguishes the two at compile time, so the device build is
byte-for-byte unaffected.

--- a/src/hotspot/os/bsd/os_bsd.cpp
+++ b/src/hotspot/os/bsd/os_bsd.cpp
@@ static char* anon_mmap(char* requested_addr, size_t bytes, bool exec) {
   // MAP_FIXED is intentionally left out, to leave existing mappings intact.
   const int flags = MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_NORESERVE | MAP_ANONYMOUS
-#ifdef __IOS__
+#if defined(__IOS__) && defined(TARGET_OS_SIMULATOR) && TARGET_OS_SIMULATOR
+      | (exec ? MAP_JIT : 0);
+#elif defined(__IOS__)
       ;
 #else
       MACOS_ONLY(| (exec ? MAP_JIT : 0));
 #endif
